WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:04.800
On the surface, Ready Player One is stuffed
with pop culture references.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.090
But a little deeper within the movie are some
crucial details and messages worth exploring.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:13.440
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm explaining the ending to Ready

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.260
Player One and that final scene between Wade
and Halliday plus what it means for a potential

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.260
sequel.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.880
If you're new here and enjoy this video, remember
to to like and subscribe for more stuff like

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:23.880
this.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.000
Of course, spoilers ahead for the movie, so
take care if you've not seen it yet.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.920
Ready Player One is set in a dystopian future
in which people escape the real world by visiting

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:38.000
the Oasis, a vast virtual reality universe
co-created by designer James Halliday and

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.390
his friend Ogden Morrow.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:43.879
But when Halliday dies, he sets a quest for
gunters to track down one easter egg that

00:00:43.879 --> 00:00:47.870
will unlock a fortune and give them complete
control of the Oasis.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:52.609
It's when Wade, aka Parzival, begins to research
Halliday's archives, that he discovers the

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:56.379
secret track behind him in the race that leads
him to the first key.

00:00:56.379 --> 00:01:00.449
Wade used Halliday's fear of going forward
to figure out he needed to go backwards.

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:06.060
This is the first example of Wade taking time
to connect with the game's creator James Halliday

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.970
which helps him to figure out the answers
he needs.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:13.340
This theme is taken forward and is crucial
to the whole movie, plus it's also how Wade

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:17.630
got that extra life coin which came in so
useful later on.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:21.570
Wade makes the next breakthrough when his
Parzival avatar teams up with Artemis and

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.329
the rest of the High Five and they work together
on the second challenge.

00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:30.090
The set-up for this is a stunning recreation
of The Shining and again to crack this challenge,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.979
the team have to understand Halliday's personal
history and failings.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:38.340
It was when Halliday gave up trying to communicate
with Kira, who he loved, that he retreated

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.560
from the world and broke up his friendship
with Ogden Morrow.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.219
At this point in the movie, it's Samantha,
aka Artemis, who has the emotional depth to

00:01:46.219 --> 00:01:50.719
properly understand the situation and she
realises that Kira needs to be freed from

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:53.670
the kind of emotional prison that Halliday
has trapped her in.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.439
Artemis realises the leap that Halliday referred
to in his clue is more than a literal leap,

00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:03.180
so in the Overlook Hotel's ballroom she jumps
over to Kira, fighting off the zombies, and

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:08.100
asks her to dance, the very thing that Halliday
couldn't bring himself to do.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.570
The final key and challenge involves a game
called Adventure.

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:16.070
Although the villainous Nolan Sorrento's team
eventually figures out how to win the game,

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.820
they find to their dismay that winning doesn't
actually get them the key.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.920
Instead, as Wade discovers, what Halliday
wanted players to learn was that "it's not

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.040
about winning, it's about playing".

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.010
Rather than triumphing at the game, Halliday
wanted the players to discover the game's

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.500
easter egg and in that way connect with the
original creator.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:39.130
By the way, in real life, Atari, which developed
the Adventure game in the 1970s, refused to

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:44.100
give credit to their game designers and programmers
and so this digital easter egg was how one

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.670
particular designer hid a credit for himself
in the game.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.240
The message that Halliday, and ultimately
Spielberg, seem to want to convey in Ready

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.420
Player One is the idea of a player forming
a bond with the inventor.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:59.600
Playing the game in this ways transcends the
limitations of simply trying to get the highest

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.030
score or winning and, instead, it means the
creator and the player can enjoy a shared

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.220
moment.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.710
In fact, the connection between characters,
audiences and the filmmaker is a recurring

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.950
theme in Spielberg's movies.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.560
Films can feel like ongoing conversations
between a director and their audience, and

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.040
can create a long-lasting sense of closeness
between a filmmaker or film and us viewers.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:27.560
Speaking recently at a Ready Player One event
at SXSW, Spielberg noted that, "When I decide

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:32.920
to make a movie, sitting in the audience with
you, I direct in the seat right next to you.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.170
And your reaction is everything."

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:39.570
But not only does Spielberg prize that strong
connection with his own audience, he also

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.780
feels that bond with the work of other filmmakers
too.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:47.810
Indeed, he's talked previously about the unforgettable
effect on him of Stanley Kubrick's 2001: A

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.960
Space Odyssey and David Lean's Lawrence of
Arabia.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.420
And it's interesting to see this idea of a
deep connection emphasised again in Ready

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:56.420
Player One.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:01.030
In fact, it's that understanding that allows
Wade to realise that if he signs Halliday's

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:05.520
contract for sole ownership of the Oasis,
he'll make the same mistake that Halliday

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.250
did when he lost his friendship with Ogden
Morrow, his former partner in Gregarious Games.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.950
And that's when Anorak reveals the secret
prize beyond the ownership of the Oasis is

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:16.950
to meet Halliday.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:21.870
Parzival enters a room with Halliday where
a young Halliday is sitting playing a video

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:22.870
game.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:27.320
In that childhood room, Halliday gives Wade
the real easter egg prize, explaining to him

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.860
that he created the Oasis because he never
felt at home in the real world and he didn't

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.440
know how to connect with people.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.160
But Halliday adds that just before he died,
he realised that although the real world was

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.270
scary to him, reality was in his words "the
only place you could get a good meal" and

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.050
the only place that's actually real.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:50.450
Like Halliday, Spielberg has often talked
about how his own fears have fuelled his filmmaking

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:54.960
over the years, providing the starting place
for many of his projects, adding that 'The

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.420
stories always vanquish the fear.'

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:01.430
And in a further echo of Halliday's words
in the film, Spielberg recently stated in

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:06.350
an interview with EW that "escape has its
pleasurable and medicinal place, but total

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:08.430
escape should not be a destination."

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:13.180
So, in the end, Wade shares control of the
Oasis with his friends, the High Five, and

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:17.420
they decide to close the Oasis two days a
week to encourage people to participate in

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.080
the real world and to connect with each other
there.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.251
And what's curious about that last scene with
Halliday is that it implies that what we're

00:05:24.251 --> 00:05:26.620
seeing is an AI version of the man.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:31.560
It's almost as if Halliday managed to duplicate
his consciousness and upload it into the Oasis

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.080
to live on and make sure an appropriate successor
was chosen to inherit the virtual world.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.300
It's an interesting theory and would also
allow for Halliday's character to return should

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.290
a sequel to the movie ever be made.

